00:12:47,940 --> 00:12:49,820
He came up with the Joy name for the brand.

268
00:12:50,420 --> 00:12:55,420
And that's when we really started to see some nice growth.

269
00:12:55,421 --> 00:13:00,300
Today, we make almost 3 billion cones a year.

270
00:13:00,301 --> 00:13:05,540
Plant manager Tyler Tupper is a 17-year Joy Cone veteran.

271
00:13:05,541 --> 00:13:09,420
We make sugar cones, waffle cones, cake cups,

272
00:13:09,421 --> 00:13:12,260
and in many different varieties.

273
00:13:12,261 --> 00:13:15,920
Their 550,000 square foot facility

274
00:13:15,921 --> 00:13:19,280
is big, very big.

275
00:13:19,281 --> 00:13:23,320
Imagine almost 10 football fields cranking out ice cream

276
00:13:23,321 --> 00:13:26,080
cones 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

277
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:33,320
It all starts with a daily delivery of flour.

278
00:13:33,321 --> 00:13:37,260
Anywhere from eight to 26 truckloads

279
00:13:37,261 --> 00:13:40,060
arrive every single week.

280
00:13:40,061 --> 00:13:43,740
Each of the silos holds about 150,000 pounds of flour.

281
00:13:43,741 --> 00:13:46,240
In total, we'll go through about 50 million pounds

282
00:13:46,241 --> 00:13:47,200
of flour each year.

283
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:54,280
From here, the flour flows into the batter room.

284
00:13:54,281 --> 00:13:57,280
So the first ingredient to be added is the brown sugar.

285
00:13:57,281 --> 00:14:00,700
We use brown sugar because it makes a much stronger cone,

286
00:14:00,701 --> 00:14:04,700
it's better flavor, and it gives it its brown color.

287
00:14:04,701 --> 00:14:07,660
In all, we go through about 7 million pounds of sugar

288
00:14:07,661 --> 00:14:10,240
annually here in this location.

289
00:14:10,241 --> 00:14:13,400
The water is then added, and then the whole mixture

290
00:14:13,401 --> 00:14:16,280
starts to get mixed together.

291
00:14:16,281 --> 00:14:18,280
Once the batter is mixed,

292
00:14:18,281 --> 00:14:23,280
it can be pumped out to any of 71 ovens in the plant.

293
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:26,280
The patties go through the oven.

294
00:14:26,281 --> 00:14:29,280
It's about 350 degrees in about 90 seconds,

295
00:14:29,281 --> 00:14:32,280
and we make about 6,000 an hour.

296
00:14:32,281 --> 00:14:34,280
At this point, when the patties come out,

297
00:14:34,281 --> 00:14:37,280
they're very soft and pliable.

298
00:14:37,281 --> 00:14:39,280
The patties are then fed into a spinner,

299
00:14:39,281 --> 00:14:43,280
which wraps the cone and gives it its form.

300
00:14:43,281 --> 00:14:46,280
At this point, the cone is at its best,

301
00:14:46,281 --> 00:14:48,780
fresh and warm.

302
00:14:48,781 --> 00:14:51,120
Now it's time to pack these babies up

303
00:14:51,121 --> 00:14:54,960
and get them headed towards your scoop of ice cream.

304
00:14:54,961 --> 00:14:59,000
And the cones are automatically packaged into a carton.

305
00:14:59,001 --> 00:15:01,960
And then it goes into an automated case packer.

306
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:07,260
It'll remain in the warehouse until we need it for shipment,

307
00:15:07,261 --> 00:15:09,000
at which point it'll be loaded onto a truck.

308
00:15:09,001 --> 00:15:11,800
It could really be shipped anywhere in the world.

309
00:15:11,801 --> 00:15:14,300
Including an ice cream parlor near you.

310
00:15:14,301 --> 00:15:16,540
If you love your ice cream on a cone,

311
00:15:16,560 --> 00:15:20,700
chances are you've had a joy cone.

312
00:15:20,740 --> 00:15:24,580
Back at Turkey Hill, it's time to drop some science

313
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:27,620
on that ice cream mix that we just made.

314
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:30,280
This baby right here, this is the pasteurization unit.

315
00:15:30,320 --> 00:15:32,360
We heat the mix up to 180.

316
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:34,560
We run it through these holding tubes.

317
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:36,360
So it holds it at that temperature.

318
00:15:36,380 --> 00:15:39,100
Holds it, and it's timed out to last 30 seconds.

319
00:15:39,120 --> 00:15:42,320
From there, we're going to send it over to the homogenizer.

320
00:15:42,321 --> 00:15:43,220
Gotcha.

321
00:15:43,221 --> 00:15:44,480
And those are those pistons.

322
00:15:44,481 --> 00:15:47,120
They're actually going to make sure that all the particles,

323
00:15:47,121 --> 00:15:50,120
the fat, the actual milk particles, the flavoring

324
00:15:50,121 --> 00:15:53,060
elements, everything is the same size.

325
00:15:53,061 --> 00:15:56,360
And that kind of evenness is what makes that creamy feeling

326
00:15:56,361 --> 00:15:57,560
in your mouth.

327
00:15:57,561 --> 00:15:59,080
And then where does it go?

328
00:15:59,081 --> 00:16:01,080
So we're going to drop it down to 34 degrees

329
00:16:01,081 --> 00:16:02,680
before we put it into a holding tank

330
00:16:02,681 --> 00:16:03,980
and take it down to ice cream production.

331
00:16:03,981 --> 00:16:05,480
And the holding tanks are just over here.

332
00:16:05,481 --> 00:16:06,320
Right out here.

333
00:16:06,321 --> 00:16:07,320
Follow me.

334
00:16:07,321 --> 00:16:08,320
Lead on.

335
00:16:08,321 --> 00:16:11,820
Well, they say money can't buy you love,

336
00:16:11,821 --> 00:16:15,660
but it can buy you ice cream, and that's almost as good.

337
00:16:15,661 --> 00:16:20,660
In fact, the world spends about $175 million

338
00:16:20,661 --> 00:16:22,960
on ice cream every single day.

339
00:16:22,961 --> 00:16:26,480
Here at Turkey Hill, we've got our premium white mix

