00:25:54,481 --> 00:25:55,480
What's up?

529
00:25:55,481 --> 00:25:56,380
What's up?

530
00:25:56,381 --> 00:26:00,480
I'm going to have you add the cookie dough flavor.

531
00:26:00,481 --> 00:26:01,980
Thank you, thank you.

532
00:26:09,420 --> 00:26:12,880
Our premium white mix still tastes like vanilla

533
00:26:12,881 --> 00:26:15,380
until we add this.

534
00:26:15,381 --> 00:26:19,380
That's the cookie dough ice cream flavor.

535
00:26:19,381 --> 00:26:20,780
That is so awesome.

536
00:26:20,781 --> 00:26:24,380
And you just made your first cookie dough ice cream mix.

537
00:26:24,381 --> 00:26:27,020
I love it, and the smell is amazing.

538
00:26:27,120 --> 00:26:29,880
Cookie dough is one of the country's favorite flavors.

539
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:33,320
In fact, it's hard to find anyone who doesn't like it.

540
00:26:33,420 --> 00:26:35,920
And you could say the same for my favorite flavor,

541
00:26:36,020 --> 00:26:38,620
mint chocolate chip, which, legend has it,

542
00:26:38,720 --> 00:26:42,040
was conceived in 1973 by a British culinary student

543
00:26:42,140 --> 00:26:44,440
who won a contest to provide ice cream

544
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:46,440
for Princess Anne's wedding.

545
00:26:46,540 --> 00:26:49,540
Or Neapolitan ice cream, which was brought to America

546
00:26:49,640 --> 00:26:53,180
by immigrants from Naples in the late 19th century.

547
00:26:54,380 --> 00:26:59,380
But other cultures have even more variety than we do,

548
00:26:59,480 --> 00:27:07,420
like squid, cow tongue, and raw horse flesh.

549
00:27:07,520 --> 00:27:14,940
To be clear, not just horse flesh, but raw horse flesh.

550
00:27:15,040 --> 00:27:19,640
You can taste all these flavors the next time you visit Tokyo.

551
00:27:19,740 --> 00:27:21,480
Not whetting your appetite?

552
00:27:21,481 --> 00:27:24,480
The fact is that coming up with new ice cream flavors

553
00:27:24,580 --> 00:27:27,180
that appeal to large swaths of the public

554
00:27:27,280 --> 00:27:29,080
is an art in and of itself.

555
00:27:31,220 --> 00:27:34,220
Welcome to an ice cream shrine very few

556
00:27:34,320 --> 00:27:36,220
have been blessed to enter.

557
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:39,240
The Dreyer's Grand Ice Cream Test Kitchen,

558
00:27:39,340 --> 00:27:42,240
where new ice cream flavors are born.

559
00:27:42,340 --> 00:27:45,240
One of the greatest ice cream flavors of all time,

560
00:27:45,340 --> 00:27:48,280
Rocky Road, was invented by founders

561
00:27:48,380 --> 00:27:51,480
William Dreyer and Joseph Eady.

562
00:27:51,481 --> 00:27:56,480
Dryer's Grand Ice Cream has been the company name since 1948.

563
00:27:56,481 --> 00:28:00,480
But in the 80s, Dryer's was expanding east of the Rockies

564
00:28:00,481 --> 00:28:05,480
into markets that were already selling Breyer's Brand Ice Cream.

565
00:28:05,481 --> 00:28:09,480
They decided to use the Edie's label in those areas,

566
00:28:09,481 --> 00:28:13,480
so their product wouldn't be confused with Breyer's.

567
00:28:13,481 --> 00:28:16,480
Today, we get a secret glimpse of the development team

568
00:28:16,481 --> 00:28:18,480
as they test out a new flavor,

569
00:28:18,481 --> 00:28:23,480
a new flavor, one that looks back to Rocky Road for inspiration.

570
00:28:23,580 --> 00:28:27,180
Dreyer and Edie added marshmallows and chopped walnuts

571
00:28:27,280 --> 00:28:31,220
to chocolate ice cream in 1929 and called their new flavor

572
00:28:31,320 --> 00:28:34,420
Rocky Road because America had just fallen into the Great

573
00:28:34,520 --> 00:28:38,240
Depression and had a Rocky Road ahead of it.

574
00:28:38,340 --> 00:28:41,940
A big reason for Rocky Road's enduring popularity

575
00:28:42,040 --> 00:28:43,840
has to do with its texture.

576
00:28:43,940 --> 00:28:47,480
The gooeyness of marshmallows is a perfect counterpoint

577
00:28:47,481 --> 00:28:49,280
to the crunch of the nuts.

578
00:28:49,281 --> 00:28:52,780
So taking that as inspiration, the team uses brownies

579
00:28:52,781 --> 00:28:55,480
to get the gooey texture similar to marshmallows.

580
00:28:55,481 --> 00:28:56,980
But what about the crunch?

581
00:28:56,981 --> 00:29:00,320
First, they try chocolate rice.

582
00:29:00,321 --> 00:29:01,820
I like the crunch a lot.

583
00:29:01,821 --> 00:29:05,640
I'm not personally sold yet that a rice crunch

584
00:29:05,641 --> 00:29:07,240
goes well with brownie.

585
00:29:07,241 --> 00:29:09,440
Next, they try toffee.

586
00:29:09,441 --> 00:29:11,240
Toffee is yummy.

587
00:29:11,241 --> 00:29:13,640
But is it taking away from the brownie?

588
00:29:13,641 --> 00:29:15,140
Yeah.

589
00:29:15,141 --> 00:29:16,980
What was that material we looked at?

590
00:29:16,981 --> 00:29:18,140
a couple of weeks ago, Agata.

591
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:19,180
Brownie brittle.

592
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:20,720
Let me have one of those mixing bowls.

593
00:29:20,820 --> 00:29:23,480
To find the right crunch for their new flavor,

594
00:29:23,580 --> 00:29:26,220
the team turns to brownie brittle.

595
00:29:26,320 --> 00:29:28,600
They mix it in with the gooey regular brownies

596
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:31,300
and also brownie batter.

597
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:32,200
Mm-hmm.

598
00:29:32,300 --> 00:29:33,100
Oh, my gosh.

599
00:29:33,200 --> 00:29:34,000
I think this is it.

600
00:29:34,100 --> 00:29:35,060
That's so good.

601
00:29:35,160 --> 00:29:39,740
This new brownie three-ways flavor is a home run.

602
00:29:39,840 --> 00:29:42,740
The next step is to refine the ingredients

603
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:45,200
and make sure the new flavor works well

604
00:29:45,201 --> 00:29:47,360
when produced on a larger scale.

605
00:29:47,361 --> 00:29:49,700
Take the brownies up just a smidge more.

606
00:29:49,701 --> 00:29:53,440
The development team will keep working on the new flavor

607
00:29:53,441 --> 00:29:56,440
until it's perfected and ready for mass production

608
00:29:56,441 --> 00:29:57,840
on the factory floor.

609
00:30:03,060 --> 00:30:05,360
Here at the Turkey Hill Ice Cream Factory,

610
00:30:05,361 --> 00:30:07,460
we just put the pedal to the metal

611
00:30:07,461 --> 00:30:10,360
and added cookie dough to our cookie dough ice cream.

612
00:30:12,960 --> 00:30:13,960
It's delicious.

613
00:30:15,200 --> 00:30:19,040
And it has like the same granularity of real cookie dough.

614
00:30:19,041 --> 00:30:21,040
That's the thing that I love.

615
00:30:21,041 --> 00:30:23,440
It doesn't taste artificial.

616
00:30:23,441 --> 00:30:27,220
You can keep your tequila, give me a cookie dough shooter.

617
00:30:27,221 --> 00:30:30,220
But I'm learning there is a lot more than just cookie dough

618
00:30:30,221 --> 00:30:32,080
in this cookie dough ice cream.

619
00:30:32,081 --> 00:30:33,880
So this is liquid chip.

620
00:30:33,881 --> 00:30:35,260
Really?

621
00:30:35,261 --> 00:30:36,680
Liquid chip?

622
00:30:36,681 --> 00:30:37,560
Liquid chip.

623
00:30:37,561 --> 00:30:39,320
Like liquid chocolate chip?

624
00:30:39,321 --> 00:30:42,320
Yeah, when it goes into the ice cream,

625
00:30:42,321 --> 00:30:45,660
It's like a little sheet going into the ice cream,

626
00:30:45,661 --> 00:30:47,660
and it's solidified.

627
00:30:47,661 --> 00:30:50,080
Oh, the cold of the ice cream hardens it.

628
00:30:50,081 --> 00:30:52,960
Yes.

629
00:30:52,961 --> 00:30:56,260
They extrude liquid warm chocolate

630
00:30:56,261 --> 00:30:58,580
into freezing cold ice cream.

631
00:30:58,581 --> 00:31:02,220
It hardens, and it breaks in the churning process.

632
00:31:02,221 --> 00:31:05,560
So you get those awesome irregular bits

633
00:31:05,561 --> 00:31:08,720
that are living, and they're a little chewy.

634
00:31:08,721 --> 00:31:11,760
It's like chocolate chip 2.0.

635
00:31:11,761 --> 00:31:13,760
It's amazing!

636
00:31:13,761 --> 00:31:16,260
Liquid chocolate chips, people.

637
00:31:16,261 --> 00:31:18,260
We are living in the future.

638
00:31:18,261 --> 00:31:21,260
Our ice cream batch is almost ready.

639
00:31:21,261 --> 00:31:25,260
And here is where it gets loaded into cartons.

640
00:31:25,261 --> 00:31:28,760
And if you follow this pipe, it goes all the way back

641
00:31:28,761 --> 00:31:32,760
to right where we were standing, where you see the cookie dough.

642
00:31:32,761 --> 00:31:35,260
The cookie dough balls go in.

643
00:31:35,261 --> 00:31:37,260
There's the chocolate.

644
00:31:37,261 --> 00:31:40,760
So we have the liquid chip, the ice cream meat,

645
00:31:40,761 --> 00:31:44,140
That cookie dough meat, the cans mixed together,

646
00:31:44,141 --> 00:31:47,640
the lids go in here, the containers come through here,

647
00:31:47,641 --> 00:31:51,840
and right here is literally where the rubber meets the road.

648
00:31:51,841 --> 00:31:55,200
Container goes down, and the ice cream goes right here.

649
00:31:55,201 --> 00:31:57,240
And look how creamy it is.

650
00:31:57,241 --> 00:32:02,120
It's actually closer to soft serve than hard scoop ice cream.

651
00:32:02,121 --> 00:32:06,760
I love when ice cream is this consistency.

652
00:32:06,761 --> 00:32:08,420
There's nothing like it in the world.

653
00:32:08,421 --> 00:32:10,260
No, this is the best way to get it.

654
00:32:10,261 --> 00:32:11,100
I agree.

655
00:32:11,101 --> 00:32:15,860
That right there is when ice cream is at its best.

656
00:32:15,861 --> 00:32:19,700
And when I die, I want you to bury me in one of these pubs.

657
00:32:22,380 --> 00:32:25,180
These cartons are destined for home freezers

658
00:32:25,181 --> 00:32:27,420
all across America.

659
00:32:27,421 --> 00:32:30,940
But before the home freezer became common in the 1940s,

660
00:32:30,941 --> 00:32:34,280
ice cream was a destination.

661
00:32:34,281 --> 00:32:36,660
And the places where we enjoyed ice cream

662
00:32:36,661 --> 00:32:39,380
became cultural touchstones, starting soon

663
00:32:39,381 --> 00:32:42,780
after the revolution in 1776 with the emergence

664
00:32:42,781 --> 00:32:46,180
of the ice cream parlor.

665
00:32:46,181 --> 00:32:50,020
Where ice cream was particularly popular

666
00:32:50,021 --> 00:32:53,020
in that sort of early post-revolutionary period

667
00:32:53,021 --> 00:32:56,040
was in these places called pleasure gardens.

668
00:32:56,041 --> 00:32:59,540
They would specialize in things like ice cream and lemonade.

669
00:32:59,541 --> 00:33:02,140
It was a relatively respectable place to go

670
00:33:02,141 --> 00:33:06,480
versus the bars and the taverns

671
00:33:06,481 --> 00:33:09,580
where no respectable woman would be seen.

672
00:33:09,581 --> 00:33:12,020
But as the 20th century dawned,

673
00:33:12,021 --> 00:33:14,820
ice cream parlors had to compete for customers

674
00:33:14,821 --> 00:33:18,860
with a new ice cream destination, the soda fountain.

675
00:33:18,861 --> 00:33:21,500
The first soda fountains actually appeared

676
00:33:21,501 --> 00:33:23,600
in drugstores and pharmacies.

677
00:33:23,601 --> 00:33:26,640
They marketed their soft drinks as health tonics

678
00:33:26,641 --> 00:33:29,480
with ingredients that truly packed a punch,

679
00:33:29,481 --> 00:33:33,480
like cocaine and the digestive enzyme pepsin,

680
00:33:33,481 --> 00:33:35,800
before they expanded their offerings.

681
00:33:35,801 --> 00:33:39,240
They came up with various kinds of new ways

682
00:33:39,241 --> 00:33:41,880
of combining soda and ice cream.

683
00:33:41,881 --> 00:33:45,040
So this is where you get that ice cream float

684
00:33:45,041 --> 00:33:47,740
that became popular throughout the 20th century.

685
00:33:49,080 --> 00:33:51,880
And with the rise of prohibition in 1920,

686
00:33:51,881 --> 00:33:55,560
soda fountain's popularity exploded even more.

687
00:33:55,561 --> 00:33:58,080
You could enjoy an ice cold Coca-Cola

688
00:33:58,081 --> 00:34:00,020
or a Haier's root beer float

689
00:34:00,021 --> 00:34:04,260
at more than 100,000 soda fountains across the US.

690
00:34:04,261 --> 00:34:05,960
Once they close the bars and the taverns,

691
00:34:05,961 --> 00:34:07,440
where are you going to socialize?

692
00:34:07,441 --> 00:34:11,360
Prohibition ended in 1933, and soda fountains

693
00:34:11,361 --> 00:34:13,640
have since waned in popularity.

694
00:34:13,641 --> 00:34:17,040
But the ice cream parlor remains a top spot

695
00:34:17,041 --> 00:34:20,820
for both high school dates and family fun two centuries on.

696
00:34:28,120 --> 00:34:32,520
We've made our mix, added our inclusions,

697
00:34:32,521 --> 00:34:36,120
And now we're finally loading up those iconic Turkey Hill

698
00:34:36,121 --> 00:34:40,360
cartons with our own batch of cookie dough ice cream.

699
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:45,300
All right, so now where are they going once they're filled?

700
00:34:45,301 --> 00:34:46,660
Where are they heading off to?

701
00:34:46,661 --> 00:34:50,280
At this point, we're going to take every container over

702
00:34:50,281 --> 00:34:54,200
and put the code date on the bottom of the container.

703
00:34:54,201 --> 00:34:58,780
So the code, so like what time it came out, what date?

704
00:34:58,781 --> 00:35:10,420
The time it was made, the expiration date, the operator code, all the information that you need to track that individual container.

705
00:35:11,120 --> 00:35:14,964
So if there's any issue, which I'm sure there never is, they can track it down.

706
00:35:14,965 --> 00:35:15,840
That's incredible.

707
00:35:17,340 --> 00:35:23,500
Then they go through a metal detector because, of course, we want to make sure that there are no foreign objects in our ice cream.

708
00:35:23,720 --> 00:35:24,740
That's a metal detector.

709
00:35:24,920 --> 00:35:25,620
Yep.

710
00:35:25,621 --> 00:35:30,140
So we saw quality control even coming from the mill.

711
00:35:32,340 --> 00:35:35,960
But even on the way out, they're getting a gate,

712
00:35:37,180 --> 00:35:40,660
a batch number, the time they were made,

713
00:35:41,820 --> 00:35:46,060
who is the operator, and then they get,

714
00:35:46,061 --> 00:35:49,340
went to a metal detector for an even extra added level

715
00:35:49,341 --> 00:35:50,180
of safety.

716
00:35:51,260 --> 00:35:53,440
So this is the best part.

717
00:35:53,441 --> 00:35:55,440
We're gonna taste the ice cream.

718
00:35:55,441 --> 00:35:56,440
Finally!

719
00:35:57,440 --> 00:35:58,440
Hallelujah!

720
00:36:00,440 --> 00:36:01,440
So this is your container.

721
00:36:01,441 --> 00:36:03,440
Oh, my goodness.

722
00:36:04,440 --> 00:36:07,440
So there's two parts to this, all right?

723
00:36:07,441 --> 00:36:09,440
One is we're gonna dip through it,

724
00:36:09,441 --> 00:36:12,440
so just make sure that we've got cookie dough pieces

725
00:36:12,441 --> 00:36:16,440
and the chocolate chip all throughout it.

726
00:36:16,441 --> 00:36:19,440
And then whatever's on the lid, you take.

727
00:36:19,441 --> 00:36:21,440
Hold it like a spoon.

728
00:36:21,441 --> 00:36:24,440
And we're going to lift through that.

729
00:36:24,441 --> 00:36:26,440
Oh, hell yeah.

730
00:36:30,440 --> 00:36:34,440
I can eat it like this right here.

731
00:36:51,440 --> 00:36:57,680
Good.

732
00:36:58,040 --> 00:36:59,380
Really, really good.

733
00:36:59,860 --> 00:37:00,580
Oh, my God.

734
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:04,740
This is unlike any ice cream you're ever going to have.

735
00:37:05,380 --> 00:37:06,400
Because you know why?

736
00:37:07,660 --> 00:37:13,200
Before you freeze it, I think you really taste the characteristic of every ingredient.

